// src/pdxb_sign.pdx — libpdx-volume.M3-001 (#7) + M3-002 (#8)
//                      + R91-XREPO.M1 Phase D (v1.1.5, satellite-runtime
//                        NO_SIGNER surfacing per satellite-runtime-shim.md §3.2)
// PdxbSign: ML-DSA-65 (FIPS 204) signing helpers for the PDXB codec
// (superblock) and, ahead of a real consumer, the inode-tail shape a
// future fsck.pdxfs will use.
//
// Three public entry points:
//
//   pdxb_sign_superblock(sb_ptr, seed_ptr, out_sig_ptr, out_sig_len) -> u64
//   pdxb_sign_inode_tail(inode_ptr, tail_offset, tail_len, seed_ptr, out_sig_ptr) -> u64
//   pdxb_verify_inode_tail(inode_ptr, tail_offset, tail_len, sig_ptr, sig_len, pub_key_slot) -> u64
//
// Both sign entry points return one of THREE distinct sentinels on
// failure:
//
//   PDXB_SIGN_ERR_NO_SIGNER (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA, -6 as u64) --
//     `paideia-satellite-runtime::mldsa65_sign_runtime_entry` (the
//     fail-closed satellite-build FFI thunk) returned -6, meaning "no
//     signer exists in this build". See PDXB_SIGN_ERR_NO_SIGNER decl
//     below and design/infrastructure/satellite-runtime-shim.md §3.2.
//     The banded equivalent for callers on the `LPV_E_*` idiom is
//     LpvErrors::LPV_E_SIGN_NO_SIGNER (0x0503).
//   PDXB_SIGN_ERR (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) -- any other non-zero return
//     from the intrinsic (kernel-build reject, invalid pointer, etc.),
//     OR (pdxb_sign_superblock only) the caller's out_sig_len is <
//     3309.
//   3309 on success (bytes written to out_sig_ptr).
//
// ================================================================
// Where `seed_ptr` comes from -- NOT a `sig_key_slot` cap, and why
// ================================================================
//
// libpdx-volume#7's brief names the signing arg `sig_key_slot` (a cap
// slot holding KIND_SIG_KEY). This file implements it as `seed_ptr` (a
// raw pointer to the 32-byte ML-DSA-65 seed) instead, for two
// independent reasons, either of which alone would force this:
//
//   1. `KIND_SIG_KEY` as landed (paideia-os
//      src/kernel/core/cap/kind_sig_key.pdx, R52.M5-001 #1703) is a
//      PUBLIC-key handle: its row holds `key_hash[32]` (a BLAKE3
//      fingerprint) and `key_material_lba` (pointing at public key
//      bytes), used at MOUNT time to cross-check the on-disk
//      `sig_key_hash` field (design/filesystem/volume-fs-substrate.md
//      §6.4, §380: "Volume mount cross-checks superblock.sig_key_hash
//      == KIND_SIG_KEY.key_hash"). Its own module header calls it "the
//      runtime authority for ONE ML-DSA-65 public-key handle used for
//      ... verification". There is no `R_SIGK_WRITE`/read-seed op and
//      no kernel capability kind anywhere in the tree that holds a
//      PRIVATE ML-DSA-65 signing seed. A "sig_key_slot" argument
//      cannot supply what `MlDsa65::sign` needs no matter how it is
//      resolved.
//   2. Even if such a capability existed, this library's own
//      established discipline (design/architecture.md §7, restated at
//      every milestone) is that it issues ZERO cap-invoke syscalls
//      anywhere -- every function operates on a caller-supplied raw
//      pointer or already-validated integer. Resolving a slot to key
//      material is inherently a cap-invoke (or a kernel-internal table
//      walk this ring-3 client cannot reach at all), which this
//      library has never done and does not start doing here.
//
// `mkfs-pdxb/main.pdx`'s own `--sig-key <path>` flag corroborates this
// reading independently: its default value
// (`tests/qemu/keys/pdxb-dev.mldsa65.pub`) is explicitly the PUBLIC
// half of the dev key pair, used only to compute `sig_key_hash` via
// BLAKE3 -- that tool has never had a route to the PRIVATE seed either.
//
// Given that, the honest, buildable primitive is "sign these bytes
// with a seed you already have resolved into memory" -- exactly what
// the `MlDsa65::sign` intrinsic itself takes. **Flagged for main:** a
// `sig_key_slot`-shaped wrapper can be layered on top of
// `pdxb_sign_superblock`/`pdxb_sign_inode_tail` once (a) a private-
// signing-key capability kind lands kernel-side and (b) this library
// (or a caller) is willing to add its first cap-invoke call to resolve
// it -- neither of which is scoped to M3.
//
// ================================================================
// The `MlDsa65::sign` intrinsic -- calling convention discovered
// ================================================================
//
// `tools/paideia-as/crates/paideia-stdlib/pdx/mldsa.pdx` (paideia-os
// submodule, landed v0.23.0 per paideia-as#1330) declares:
//
//   trait MlDsa65 {
//     fn sign(seed_ptr: u64, msg_ptr: u64, msg_len: u64, sig_out_ptr: u64)
//       -> i64 !{crypto, mem} @{paideia.crypto};
//   }
//
// SysV register mapping (mirrored exactly from that file's doc comment
// and from `paideia-pq-sign::ffi::mldsa65_sign_runtime_entry`, the
// extern-C thunk the elaborator's `stdlib_lowering::mldsaops` recipe
// routes the call to):
//
//   RDI  seed_ptr     *const u8, 32-byte ML-DSA-65 seed (MLDSA65_SK_LEN)
//   RSI  msg_ptr      *const u8
//   RDX  msg_len      u64
//   RCX  sig_out_ptr  *mut u8, >= 3309 bytes (MLDSA65_SIG_LEN)
//   RAX  return code  i64: 0 = OK (signature written to sig_out_ptr[..3309]);
//                     -1 = InvalidParam (a required pointer was NULL);
//                     -2 = Length (defensive; should be unreachable).
//                     On any non-zero return, sig_out_ptr is untouched.
//
// A repo-wide grep at this landing found ZERO real (non-test) `.pdx`
// call sites for `MlDsa65::sign` anywhere in paideia-os or paideia-as
// -- `superblock_write.pdx` (paideia-os) explicitly DESCOPED calling it
// ("the R52 blocker gap"), and `mkfs-pdxb/main.pdx` still leaves `sig`
// all-zero. This file's `pdxb_sign_mldsa65_raw` is, as far as this
// landing's research could establish, the FIRST real caller of this
// intrinsic in the tree. The only precedent for the call-site SYNTAX
// itself is a regression-test fixture,
// `tools/paideia-as/tests/build-emit/trait_call_lambda_body.pdx`
// (paideia-as#1290), which shows a trait declared locally inside the
// SAME module that calls it, then invoked as a plain (non-`unsafe`,
// non-`block`) lambda body:
//
//   trait PerCpuOps { fn write_u64(a: u64, b: u64) -> () !{...} @{...}; }
//   pub let wrap : (u64, u64) -> () !{...} @{...} =
//     fn(a: u64, b: u64) -> PerCpuOps::write_u64(a, b)
//
// This module follows that precedent exactly: the elaborator's
// `stdlib_lowering::lower_stdlib_method` dispatches on the LITERAL
// `(trait_name, method_name)` strings at the call site
// (`crates/paideia-as-elaborator/src/stdlib_lowering/mod.rs`), not on
// a resolved/imported trait definition -- so a module-local
// declaration of `trait MlDsa65 { fn sign(...) -> i64 ...; }` is
// exactly enough for the elaborator to recognize and lower a
// `MlDsa65::sign(...)` call in this module, with no cross-crate import
// mechanism needed or available. **Flagged for main:** confirm this
// reading holds once this file first reaches a real paideia-as build
// (`bash tools/build.sh` is main-only per this repo's own discipline;
// this landing does not run it).
//
// A raw `unsafe { block: {...} }` body (the mnemonic-sequence grammar
// every other function in this repo uses) is NOT the right home for a
// trait-call expression -- the fixture above shows trait calls only at
// a lambda-body / tail-expression position, a different grammar than
// the instruction list inside `block: {...}`. `pdxb_sign_mldsa65_raw`
// is therefore its OWN plain (non-`unsafe`) function whose entire body
// is the `MlDsa65::sign(...)` call; every public entry point in this
// file reaches it via an ordinary `call pdxb_sign_mldsa65_raw;` from
// inside its own `unsafe` block, exactly like calling any other
// sibling function in this codebase (e.g. `kind_sig_key.pdx`'s
// `sig_key_row_key_type` calling `sig_key_tail_valid`).
//
// ================================================================
// Signature coverage -- bytes [0, 696) of the superblock
// ================================================================
//
// design/filesystem/volume-fs-substrate.md §2.1 (paideia-os) states
// this in two places, both agreeing:
//
//   | 696 | 3400 | sig | u8[3400] | ML-DSA-65 signature covering bytes
//                                    [0, 696). (3309 B sig + 91 B slack.) |
//   "sig scope is bytes [0, 696). This excludes sig itself and excludes
//    _reserved. Adding a field means bumping version and re-writing sig."
//
// `pdxb_sign_superblock` therefore signs exactly `sb_ptr[0, 696)` --
// the fixed-field region PLUS `_reserved` [160, 696), NOT just the 176
// on-disk bytes this library's own `PdxbSuperblockSummary` decodes
// (see `src/pdxb_superblock.pdx` / design/architecture.md §3: the
// summary deliberately excludes `_reserved` and `sig`, but the SIGNED
// RANGE is a wire-format fact independent of what this library's
// in-memory struct happens to decode). `PDXB_SIGN_MSG_LEN` (696) below
// is this file's own copy of `PdxbSuperblock::PDXB_D_OFF_SIG` --
// duplicated rather than imported because this repo has no established
// cross-module constant-import syntax exercised anywhere yet; both
// constants must be kept in sync if the wire format ever changes
// (flagged here so a future edit to one is not made without the
// other).
//
// ================================================================
// Where the signature actually lands -- caller's choice, not a
// hardcoded sb_ptr+696 write
// ================================================================
//
// The issue brief says both "out_sig_ptr: buffer to receive signature"
// AND "the function should write the signature into
// sb_ptr[696..696+3309] (overwriting whatever was there)". This file
// reads those as ONE contract, not two: `pdxb_sign_superblock` signs
// `sb_ptr[0, 696)` and writes the result to wherever `out_sig_ptr`
// points -- a plain out-pointer, matching every other function in this
// library (design/architecture.md §2). A caller that wants "sign in
// place, overwriting the superblock's own sig region" passes
// `out_sig_ptr = sb_ptr + 696` (and `out_sig_len >= 3309`); a caller
// building M4's fuzz/round-trip fixtures can instead pass an
// independent scratch buffer to check the signature without mutating
// the encoded superblock under test. This is deliberately the more
// general of the two readings and costs nothing -- the function never
// needs to know `sb_ptr`'s own total size beyond the fixed 696-byte
// signed prefix. **Flagged for main:** confirm this reading; if
// `pdxb_sign_superblock` should instead ALWAYS self-target
// `sb_ptr + 696` regardless of `out_sig_ptr` (ignoring or dropping that
// argument), that is a one-line change (replace the `out_sig_ptr`
// register source with `sb_ptr + 696` computed via the same
// mov+add-no-lea pattern `pdxb_sign_inode_tail` already uses below) but
// is a real API-shape decision, not a bug fix.
//
// ================================================================
// `mldsa65_verify` -- does not exist; `pdxb_verify_inode_tail` is a
// STUB
// ================================================================
//
// A repo-wide grep for `mldsa65_verify` / `MlDsa65Verify` / a `verify`
// method on the `MlDsa65` trait across both `paideia-os` and its
// `tools/paideia-as` submodule (v0.24.0) found nothing: `mldsa.pdx`
// declares `sign` only, and `paideia-pq-sign::ffi` exports only
// `mldsa65_sign_runtime_entry`. `pdxb_verify_inode_tail` is therefore a
// scaffold STUB per this file's own M1-stub precedent elsewhere in this
// library (vol_kind_mint, the M1 forms of pdxb_parse/encode_superblock):
// it dereferences NONE of its arguments and always returns
// `PDXB_VER_ERR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED` (a nonzero sentinel) rather than ever
// claiming a signature is valid it cannot actually check -- fail-closed,
// not fail-open. Once a `verify` method lands on the `MlDsa65` trait
// (or a sibling trait), this function's real body will mirror
// `pdxb_sign_inode_tail`'s shape: marshal `(sig_ptr, msg_ptr, msg_len,
// pub_key_bytes_ptr)` or similar into the new intrinsic's registers and
// forward its return code. Unlike the two sign helpers, `pub_key_slot`
// here is NOT a naming mismatch the way `sig_key_slot` was for signing
// -- `KIND_SIG_KEY`'s landed shape genuinely IS a public-key handle, so
// a future real body could plausibly resolve it once this library (or
// its caller) is willing to add a cap-invoke call; that is a separate
// decision from today's stub, which resolves nothing.
//
// ================================================================
// `pdxb_sign_inode_tail` -- no `out_sig_len` gate (matches the issue's
// literal 5-arg signature)
// ================================================================
//
// Unlike `pdxb_sign_superblock`, the issue brief's
// `pdxb_sign_inode_tail(inode_ptr, tail_offset, tail_len, sig_key_slot,
// out_sig_ptr)` has no output-capacity argument. This file implements
// exactly that 5-arg shape (with `seed_ptr` replacing `sig_key_slot`
// per the rationale above) rather than silently adding a 6th argument.
// **Flagged for main:** the caller MUST guarantee `out_sig_ptr` has at
// least `MLDSA65_SIG_LEN` (3309) writable bytes -- there is no gate
// here to catch a too-small buffer, unlike every other output-buffer
// argument in this library. `fsck.pdxfs` (the eventual consumer, not
// yet written) should either always pass a fixed 3309+-byte scratch
// buffer or this signature should grow a 6th `out_sig_len` argument
// before a real consumer lands.
//
// paideia-as encoder notes for this file:
//   - No `add reg, [mem]` anywhere. Pointer arithmetic
//     (`msg_ptr = inode_ptr + tail_offset`) uses `mov dest, regA;
//     add dest, regB` (register-to-register), never
//     `lea reg, [regA + regB]` -- matching
//     `src/kernel/core/fs/pdxfs_lite/inode.pdx`'s `get_inode` precedent
//     (paideia-os), which explicitly avoids two-register `lea` the same
//     way.
//   - No byte loads anywhere in this file (every value handled is a
//     full pointer or a full 64-bit length/return code), so the
//     xor-then-mov_b zero-extend pattern does not apply here.
//   - No `test` instruction; every check is `cmp`.
//   - Every function below keeps its normal call-frame discipline.
//   - Label prefixes: `pdxb_sign_ss_` (sign superblock),
//     `pdxb_sign_it_` (sign inode tail). `pdxb_verify_inode_tail` has
//     no internal branches at this stub landing, so it carries no
//     labels; a real body will use `pdxb_ver_it_`.
//
// Upstream: design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md §9.4 (libpdx-volume#7,
// #8); design/filesystem/volume-fs-substrate.md §2.1, §6.4 (paideia-os);
// tools/paideia-as/crates/paideia-stdlib/pdx/mldsa.pdx,
// tools/paideia-as/crates/paideia-as-elaborator/src/stdlib_lowering/
// mldsaops.rs, tools/paideia-as/crates/paideia-pq-sign/src/ffi.rs
// (paideia-as submodule, v0.24.0); src/kernel/core/cap/kind_sig_key.pdx
// (paideia-os, R52.M5-001 #1703).

module PdxbSign = structure {

  // --- ML-DSA-65 (FIPS 204) fixed sizes (paideia-pq-sign crate constants,
  // mirrored here since this library has no dependency on that crate's
  // Rust-side constant, only on the extern-C thunk's documented contract). ---
  pub let MLDSA65_SIG_LEN  : u64 = 3309   // MLDSA65_SIG_LEN (paideia-pq-sign)
  pub let MLDSA65_SEED_LEN : u64 = 32     // MLDSA65_SK_LEN (paideia-pq-sign); documentation only, not length-checked here (the intrinsic always reads exactly 32 bytes at seed_ptr regardless of any length this library could pass)

  // --- Superblock signed-range length (volume-fs-substrate.md §2.1's
  // "sig scope is bytes [0, 696)"). Own copy of
  // PdxbSuperblock::PDXB_D_OFF_SIG (src/pdxb_superblock.pdx) -- see
  // module header for why this is duplicated rather than imported. ---
  pub let PDXB_SIGN_MSG_LEN : u64 = 696

  // --- Failure sentinels. Same "obviously invalid u64::MAX" convention
  // every other module in this library uses (PDXB_ENC_ERR_BUF_TOO_SHORT,
  // LPV_STUB_NOT_IMPLEMENTED). ---
  pub let PDXB_SIGN_ERR              : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
  pub let PDXB_VER_ERR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

  // --- Distinct fail-closed-signer sentinel (satellite-runtime-shim
  // §3.2 + Step 6; R91-XREPO.M1 Phase D). ---
  //
  // `paideia-satellite-runtime::mldsa65_sign_runtime_entry` (the FFI
  // thunk `MlDsa65::sign` lowers to in a satellite build) is a
  // fail-closed stub that ALWAYS returns
  // `PDX_MLDSA_ERR_NO_SIGNER = -6` -- a new error code the shim added
  // deliberately outside the intrinsic's own preexisting `-1..-5` band
  // so a caller can distinguish "there is no signer in this build" from
  // "the intrinsic was called and rejected its inputs". In satellite
  // ELFs (mkfs.pdxfs, mount.pdxfs, umount.pdxfs, and any future
  // fsck.pdxfs that has not yet been retargeted at the full
  // paideia-pq-sign staticlib) this is the ONLY value the intrinsic
  // ever returns; in a kernel build it never returns this value.
  //
  // `pdxb_sign_superblock` and `pdxb_sign_inode_tail` translate this
  // -6 upward into `PDXB_SIGN_ERR_NO_SIGNER` (below), a value
  // BAND-DISTINCT from the generic `PDXB_SIGN_ERR` sentinel so the
  // caller's own logging (or `LpvErrors::lpv_strerror` dispatch) can
  // surface "sign: no signer in this build" specifically rather than
  // misreading the fail-closed stub as an intrinsic crash. The u64
  // value is -6's two's-complement bit pattern
  // (`0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA`), chosen so that a caller comparing the
  // raw returned u64 against this constant sees the same low bits as
  // the underlying -6 the runtime shim reports upward -- keeping the
  // low-level and the library-level idioms legible without either
  // needing to know the other's numeric form. Callers preferring the
  // banded `LPV_E_*` namespace can use
  // `LpvErrors::LPV_E_SIGN_NO_SIGNER` (0x0503) directly; the two
  // constants coexist for backwards compatibility with the v1.1.4
  // sentinel-comparison idiom.
  //
  // See design/infrastructure/satellite-runtime-shim.md §3.2
  // ("Consequence for libpdx-volume") and its §5 Step 6, plus §8 R4
  // ("fail-closed signer masquerading as success" -- the mitigation
  // this constant is half of).
  pub let PDXB_SIGN_ERR_NO_SIGNER : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA

  // ==========================================================================
  // Local trait declaration for the paideia-as `MlDsa65::sign` stdlib
  // intrinsic (paideia-as#1330, landed v0.23.0). See module header
  // "The MlDsa65::sign intrinsic" section for why a module-local
  // declaration (rather than an import) is the established pattern the
  // one known precedent (trait_call_lambda_body.pdx, paideia-as#1290)
  // uses, and why this is the FIRST real (non-test) call site for this
  // intrinsic found anywhere in the tree at this landing.
  // ==========================================================================
  trait MlDsa65 {
    fn sign(seed_ptr: u64, msg_ptr: u64, msg_len: u64, sig_out_ptr: u64)
      -> i64 !{crypto, mem} @{paideia.crypto};
  }

  // ==========================================================================
  // pdxb_sign_mldsa65_raw(seed_ptr, msg_ptr, msg_len, sig_out_ptr) -> i64
  //
  // Internal (not part of this issue's public API) plain lambda-body
  // wrapper -- the ONLY way to reach the trait-call lowering recipe is a
  // call expression at a lambda-body/tail-expression position; a raw
  // `unsafe { block: {...} }` body is a mnemonic sequence, not a general
  // expression grammar, so `MlDsa65::sign(...)` cannot be spliced
  // directly into `pdxb_sign_superblock`'s / `pdxb_sign_inode_tail`'s own
  // asm blocks below. Both public entry points reach this by an ordinary
  // `call pdxb_sign_mldsa65_raw;` after marshalling SysV argument
  // registers themselves, exactly like calling any other sibling
  // function in this codebase. Return type is `i64` (not this library's
  // usual `u64` status-code convention) because it must match the
  // `MlDsa65::sign` trait method's own declared return type exactly.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let pdxb_sign_mldsa65_raw : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> i64 !{crypto, mem} @{paideia.crypto} =
    fn (seed_ptr: u64, msg_ptr: u64, msg_len: u64, sig_out_ptr: u64) ->
      MlDsa65::sign(seed_ptr, msg_ptr, msg_len, sig_out_ptr)

  // ==========================================================================
  // pdxb_sign_superblock(sb_ptr, seed_ptr, out_sig_ptr, out_sig_len) -> u64
  //   rdi = sb_ptr       (4096-byte encoded superblock; only [0,696) is read)
  //   rsi = seed_ptr     (32-byte ML-DSA-65 seed -- see module header for
  //                       why this is a raw pointer, not a sig_key_slot cap)
  //   rdx = out_sig_ptr  (destination for the 3309-byte signature; caller's
  //                       choice -- pass sb_ptr + 696 to sign in place)
  //   rcx = out_sig_len  (caller-declared writable length at out_sig_ptr)
  //
  // Gate: out_sig_len >= MLDSA65_SIG_LEN (3309), else PDXB_SIGN_ERR
  // without calling the intrinsic or touching out_sig_ptr. On the
  // intrinsic returning nonzero, no partial output is produced (the
  // intrinsic's own contract: "on any error the output buffer is not
  // written"); the specific nonzero value is translated to one of two
  // sentinels per the R91-XREPO.M1 Phase D discrimination (module
  // header):
  //   * -6 (PDX_MLDSA_ERR_NO_SIGNER, the fail-closed
  //     paideia-satellite-runtime signer stub in a satellite build) ->
  //     PDXB_SIGN_ERR_NO_SIGNER (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA).
  //   * every other non-zero value (NULL seed_ptr per the intrinsic's
  //     own contract, or a real kernel-build reject) -> the generic
  //     PDXB_SIGN_ERR (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF).
  // This function does not null-check seed_ptr itself, matching this
  // library's established "trust the caller-declared pointer" discipline
  // for pdxb_parse_superblock/pdxb_encode_superblock. On success,
  // returns MLDSA65_SIG_LEN (3309), the bytes written.
  //
  // No push/pop: nothing needs to survive across `call
  // pdxb_sign_mldsa65_raw` except its i64 return code in rax, so r10/r11
  // (caller-save, clobbered by the call) are used freely as scratch
  // before the call and never read after it.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let pdxb_sign_superblock : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{crypto, mem} @{paideia.crypto} =
    fn (sb_ptr: u64) (seed_ptr: u64) (out_sig_ptr: u64) (out_sig_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {crypto, mem}, capabilities: {paideia.crypto},
      justification: "libpdx-volume.M3-001 (#7) + R91-XREPO.M1 Phase D (satellite-runtime-shim §3.2 + Step 6): pdxb_sign_superblock. Gate: cmp rcx,3309 (MLDSA65_SIG_LEN); jb too_short -- returns PDXB_SIGN_ERR without calling the intrinsic. Body: marshal SysV args for MlDsa65::sign(seed_ptr, msg_ptr, msg_len, sig_out_ptr) -- new rdi=seed_ptr (old rsi), new rsi=msg_ptr=sb_ptr (old rdi), new rdx=696 (PDXB_SIGN_MSG_LEN, the volume-fs-substrate.md sec2.1 signed range [0,696)), new rcx=sig_out_ptr (old rdx) -- staged through r10 (saved sb_ptr)/r11 (saved out_sig_ptr) since rdi and rdx's old values are each needed after their register slot is overwritten by the other's new value. call pdxb_sign_mldsa65_raw; cmp rax,0; je ok (rax=3309, ret). Non-zero return path: check for PDX_MLDSA_ERR_NO_SIGNER (i64 -6 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA, the fail-closed satellite-runtime-shim signer stub -- see the PDXB_SIGN_ERR_NO_SIGNER decl in the module header) first, staged into r10 via MOVABS then compared register-to-register (matches pdxb_crypto.pdx::pdxb_dek_unwrap's own -3 tag-mismatch discrimination pattern); je no_signer path returns PDXB_SIGN_ERR_NO_SIGNER (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA) unchanged. Any other non-zero: return the generic PDXB_SIGN_ERR (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF). This surfacing is the §3.2 R4 mitigation (no fail-closed signer masquerading as success). No push/pop -- nothing survives the call except rax. No add reg,[mem]. No byte loads. No test; every check is cmp. Labels prefixed pdxb_sign_ss_.",
      block: {
        cmp rcx, 3309;
        jb  pdxb_sign_ss_too_short;

        // Stage sb_ptr and out_sig_ptr before rdi/rdx are overwritten.
        mov r10, rdi;                    // r10 = sb_ptr (will become msg_ptr)
        mov r11, rdx;                    // r11 = out_sig_ptr (will become sig_out_ptr)

        mov rdi, rsi;                    // rdi = seed_ptr
        mov rsi, r10;                    // rsi = msg_ptr (sb_ptr)
        mov rdx, 696;                    // rdx = msg_len (PDXB_SIGN_MSG_LEN)
        mov rcx, r11;                    // rcx = sig_out_ptr

        call pdxb_sign_mldsa65_raw;

        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdxb_sign_ss_nonzero;

        mov rax, 3309;                   // MLDSA65_SIG_LEN: bytes written
        ret;

      pdxb_sign_ss_nonzero:
        // Distinguish the fail-closed satellite-runtime signer stub
        // (PDX_MLDSA_ERR_NO_SIGNER = -6 as u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA)
        // from every other non-zero intrinsic return. See the
        // PDXB_SIGN_ERR_NO_SIGNER decl in this module's header and
        // design/infrastructure/satellite-runtime-shim.md §3.2 + §8 R4.
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA;     // PDX_MLDSA_ERR_NO_SIGNER as u64
        cmp rax, r10;
        je  pdxb_sign_ss_no_signer;

        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;     // PDXB_SIGN_ERR (generic)
        ret;

      pdxb_sign_ss_no_signer:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA;     // PDXB_SIGN_ERR_NO_SIGNER
        ret;

      pdxb_sign_ss_too_short:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;     // PDXB_SIGN_ERR
        ret;
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // pdxb_sign_inode_tail(inode_ptr, tail_offset, tail_len, seed_ptr, out_sig_ptr) -> u64
  //   rdi = inode_ptr    (base of an inode block)
  //   rsi = tail_offset  (byte offset of the tail region within the block)
  //   rdx = tail_len     (byte length of the tail region -- becomes msg_len)
  //   rcx = seed_ptr     (32-byte ML-DSA-65 seed -- see module header)
  //   r8  = out_sig_ptr  (destination for the 3309-byte signature; caller
  //                       MUST guarantee >= MLDSA65_SIG_LEN writable bytes
  //                       -- see module header, no out_sig_len arg here)
  //
  // Deferred consumer (fsck.pdxfs does not exist yet; libpdx-volume#8).
  // No length gate on out_sig_ptr (matches the issue's literal 5-arg
  // signature -- flagged in the module header). Failure sentinels match
  // pdxb_sign_superblock's Phase D three-return-value contract (module
  // header): -6 -> PDXB_SIGN_ERR_NO_SIGNER (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA), any
  // other non-zero -> the generic PDXB_SIGN_ERR (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF).
  // On success, returns MLDSA65_SIG_LEN (3309).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let pdxb_sign_inode_tail : (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{crypto, mem} @{paideia.crypto} =
    fn (inode_ptr: u64) (tail_offset: u64) (tail_len: u64) (seed_ptr: u64) (out_sig_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {crypto, mem}, capabilities: {paideia.crypto},
      justification: "libpdx-volume.M3-002 (#8) + R91-XREPO.M1 Phase D (satellite-runtime-shim §3.2 + Step 6): pdxb_sign_inode_tail, deferred-consumer helper for a future fsck.pdxfs. msg_ptr = inode_ptr + tail_offset via mov r9,rdi; add r9,rsi (register+register pointer arithmetic, never lea reg,[regA+regB] -- matches pdxfs_lite/inode.pdx's get_inode precedent). Marshal SysV args for MlDsa65::sign(seed_ptr, msg_ptr, msg_len, sig_out_ptr): new rdi=seed_ptr (old rcx), new rsi=msg_ptr (r9), new rdx=msg_len=tail_len (old rdx, unchanged -- already in the right slot), new rcx=sig_out_ptr (old r8). call pdxb_sign_mldsa65_raw; cmp rax,0; je ok (rax=3309, ret). Non-zero return path mirrors pdxb_sign_superblock's Phase D discrimination: check for PDX_MLDSA_ERR_NO_SIGNER (i64 -6 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA, the fail-closed satellite-runtime-shim signer stub) first, staged into r10 via MOVABS then compared register-to-register (matches pdxb_crypto.pdx::pdxb_dek_unwrap's own -3 tag-mismatch discrimination pattern); je no_signer path returns PDXB_SIGN_ERR_NO_SIGNER (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA) unchanged. Any other non-zero: return the generic PDXB_SIGN_ERR (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF). No push/pop. No add reg,[mem] beyond the one register+register add computing msg_ptr (not a memory operand). No byte loads. No test; every check is cmp. Labels prefixed pdxb_sign_it_.",
      block: {
        // msg_ptr = inode_ptr + tail_offset.
        mov r9, rdi;                     // r9 = inode_ptr
        add r9, rsi;                     // r9 = inode_ptr + tail_offset = msg_ptr

        mov rdi, rcx;                    // rdi = seed_ptr
        mov rsi, r9;                     // rsi = msg_ptr
        // rdx already holds tail_len == msg_len; no move needed.
        mov rcx, r8;                     // rcx = sig_out_ptr

        call pdxb_sign_mldsa65_raw;

        cmp rax, 0;
        jne pdxb_sign_it_nonzero;

        mov rax, 3309;                   // MLDSA65_SIG_LEN: bytes written
        ret;

      pdxb_sign_it_nonzero:
        // Distinguish the fail-closed satellite-runtime signer stub
        // (PDX_MLDSA_ERR_NO_SIGNER = -6 as u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA)
        // from every other non-zero intrinsic return. See the
        // PDXB_SIGN_ERR_NO_SIGNER decl in this module's header and
        // design/infrastructure/satellite-runtime-shim.md §3.2 + §8 R4.
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA;     // PDX_MLDSA_ERR_NO_SIGNER as u64
        cmp rax, r10;
        je  pdxb_sign_it_no_signer;

        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;     // PDXB_SIGN_ERR (generic)
        ret;

      pdxb_sign_it_no_signer:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFA;     // PDXB_SIGN_ERR_NO_SIGNER
        ret;
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // pdxb_verify_inode_tail(inode_ptr, tail_offset, tail_len, sig_ptr, sig_len, pub_key_slot) -> u64
  //
  // STUB -- see module header "mldsa65_verify -- does not exist" section.
  // No `MlDsa65::verify` (or any verify) intrinsic exists in paideia-as
  // v0.24.0 (repo-wide grep for mldsa65_verify / MlDsa65Verify came back
  // empty). Dereferences NONE of its six arguments; always returns
  // PDXB_VER_ERR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED (nonzero -- fail-closed, never claims a
  // signature is valid it cannot check). No labels at this stub landing.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let pdxb_verify_inode_tail : (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (inode_ptr: u64) (tail_offset: u64) (tail_len: u64) (sig_ptr: u64) (sig_len: u64) (pub_key_slot: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-volume.M3-002 (#8): pdxb_verify_inode_tail STUB. No mldsa65_verify intrinsic exists in paideia-as v0.24.0 (repo-wide grep empty) -- see module header. Dereferences none of its six args; unconditionally returns PDXB_VER_ERR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF), a nonzero sentinel so a caller checking 'return == 0 means valid' never mistakes this stub for a real pass. No labels; single-path body.",
      block: {
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;     // PDXB_VER_ERR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED
        ret;
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // pdxb_verify_superblock(sb_ptr, sig_ptr, sig_len, pub_key_slot) -> u64
  //
  // libpdx-volume.M1-001 (#16). Symmetric counterpart to
  // pdxb_sign_superblock: verifies an ML-DSA-65 signature over
  // sb_ptr[0, PDXB_SIGN_MSG_LEN=696) against a pub_key_slot resolved
  // by (or provided to) the caller.
  //
  // STUB at this landing -- fail-closed, matches pdxb_verify_inode_tail's
  // discipline above and for the same reason: no mldsa65_verify
  // intrinsic exists on the MlDsa65 trait in paideia-as v0.28.0
  // (grepped tools/paideia-as/crates/paideia-stdlib/pdx/mldsa.pdx; only
  // `sign` is declared). Dereferences NONE of its four arguments.
  // Always returns PDXB_VER_ERR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF).
  //
  // The stub deliberately exists so downstream mount.pdxfs / fsck.pdxfs
  // can code against the final signature today and get a fail-closed
  // (never fail-open) result until the real body lands. Once
  // mldsa65_verify arrives on the MlDsa65 trait, this becomes a
  // straight port of pdxb_verify_inode_tail's real-body pattern:
  // marshal (sig_ptr, sig_len, msg_ptr=sb_ptr, msg_len=696,
  // pub_key_bytes_ptr) into the new intrinsic's registers and forward
  // its return code. `pub_key_slot` becomes cap-invoke-resolvable at
  // that point since KIND_SIG_KEY's landed shape is exactly the
  // public-key handle a verify path needs (design/architecture.md §8.4).
  //
  // Follow-up filed as paideia-os/paideia-as issue for the missing
  // mldsa65_verify intrinsic (tracked at the paideia-as-encoder issue
  // list; the M3-002 landing already documents the gap, and this M1-001
  // scaffold extends it to the superblock path).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let pdxb_verify_superblock : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (sb_ptr: u64) (sig_ptr: u64) (sig_len: u64) (pub_key_slot: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-volume.M1-001 (#16): pdxb_verify_superblock STUB. No mldsa65_verify intrinsic exists on the MlDsa65 trait in paideia-as v0.28.0 (grepped tools/paideia-as/crates/paideia-stdlib/pdx/mldsa.pdx -- only `sign` is declared) -- see module header 'mldsa65_verify -- does not exist' section (already documented at M3-002 landing for pdxb_verify_inode_tail). Dereferences NONE of its four arguments (sb_ptr, sig_ptr, sig_len, pub_key_slot); unconditionally returns PDXB_VER_ERR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, a nonzero sentinel via MOVABS since the value exceeds the 32-bit imm bound) so a caller checking `return == 0 means valid` never mistakes the stub for a real pass -- fail-closed by construction, matching pdxb_verify_inode_tail's discipline. No labels; single-path body. Real body will land once mldsa65_verify exists as a straight port of pdxb_verify_inode_tail's shape.",
      block: {
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;     // PDXB_VER_ERR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED
        ret;
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // pdxb_sign_superblock_traced(sb_ptr, seed_ptr, out_sig_ptr,
  //                             out_sig_len, audit_client_slot,
  //                             audit_record_kind) -> u64
  //
  // libpdx-volume.M2-002 (#22). Strict superset of pdxb_sign_superblock
  // that ALSO posts an audit record (INTENT before the sign, RESULT
  // after) via libpdx-audit's `audit_client_emit` when
  // `audit_client_slot != 0`. When `audit_client_slot == 0`, this
  // function is byte-for-byte equivalent to pdxb_sign_superblock -- no
  // cap-invoke is issued at all.
  //
  // At this landing the audit-emit branch is a STUB: libpdx-audit is
  // not yet linked into this repo's build (its `audit_client_emit`
  // extern symbol would be an unresolved reference at consumer link
  // time). Once libpdx-audit lands as a build-tree sibling, the two
  // stub arms below become ordinary cross-module calls following the
  // same convention pdxb_sign_ss_* uses for pdxb_sign_mldsa65_raw.
  //
  // Follow-up filed as paideia-os/libpdx-audit issue for the
  // build-tree wiring (this ticket lands the outer contract; audit
  // link is the last-mile step).
  //
  // Phase D (v1.1.5): the passthrough arm calls `pdxb_sign_superblock`
  // and returns its rax verbatim, so the Phase D three-sentinel
  // contract (module header) -- including
  // `PDXB_SIGN_ERR_NO_SIGNER` on the satellite-runtime fail-closed
  // stub -- surfaces through this entry point automatically. The
  // audit-stub arm still returns 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (== the generic
  // `PDXB_SIGN_ERR`) since libpdx-audit is not yet linked; this is a
  // preexisting collision with the generic sign sentinel that this
  // ticket does not attempt to disambiguate.
  //
  // Registers: rdi..r9 hold the six SysV args on entry. rax = return.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let pdxb_sign_superblock_traced : (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{crypto, mem} @{paideia.crypto} =
    fn (sb_ptr: u64) (seed_ptr: u64) (out_sig_ptr: u64) (out_sig_len: u64) (audit_client_slot: u64) (audit_record_kind: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {crypto, mem}, capabilities: {paideia.crypto},
      justification: "libpdx-volume.M2-002 (#22): pdxb_sign_superblock_traced. If audit_client_slot (r8) is 0, forward straight to pdxb_sign_superblock(rdi,rsi,rdx,rcx). Else this is currently a stub-arm (libpdx-audit is not yet linked into this repo's build tree; the extern audit_client_emit symbol would be an unresolved reference at consumer link time). At libpdx-audit-link landing, both arms of this dispatch become ordinary cross-module `call audit_client_emit` sequences bracketing the sign call. Gate: cmp r8,0; je passthrough (drops through to pdxb_sign_superblock's 4-arg call using rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx as-is). Else: mov rax,0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (LPV_STUB / not-yet-linked sentinel); ret. Passthrough branch: `call pdxb_sign_superblock;` and let its return flow through in rax. No push/pop needed (r8/r9 are caller-save under SysV; the callee doesn't need or clobber the audit args). No add reg,[mem]; only cmp reg,imm. Labels prefixed pdxb_sign_st_.",
      block: {
        cmp r8, 0;
        jne pdxb_sign_st_audit_stub;

        // audit_client_slot == 0: passthrough to the untraced helper.
        call pdxb_sign_superblock;
        ret;

      pdxb_sign_st_audit_stub:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;     // LPV_STUB / audit-link pending
        ret;
      }
    }
}
